<s>\begin{slide}

\begin{center}
{\Large Tasks and Schedulers in Real-Time Systems}
\end{center}

%%%%** Section 1 
\section{Tasks, Processes, and Threads}
%%%%** Section 1.1
\subsection{Processor Context}

The CPU of a computer has several state variables.  Once they are
specified we know the exact state of the CPU.  These include:
\begin{itemize}
        \item Program Counter (location in memory of next instruction)
        \item Value of each register
        \item Processor Status Flags
        \item Stack Pointer
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2
\subsection{Tasks or Processes}

A {\it Task} or {\it Process} is a unit of code and data which is
described by one processor context when it is running.

Examples:  {\it The Tasks of Labs 2-5}

A task is usually implemented in {\tt C} by writing a function.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\paragraph{Types of Tasks:}

%%%%** Section 1.2.1 
\subsubsection{Periodic Tasks}\label{Periodic}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in  Hard-Real time applications
        \item Examples: 1) Control: Every 10 ms., Read sensors $\to$ compute control $\to$ output
command\\
          2) Multimedia: Every 22.727$\mu$sec,  Get music sample $\to$ compute DSP filter $\to$ output
sample to DAC.

        \item Characterized by three attributes:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item $P$, Period: the regular time interval between runs of this task.
                        \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does it require
each time. Obviously $C <= P$. ($C$ may not be the same each time.
<*chn>
Different code branches in the task may take different amounts of time. Use $C=C_{max}$.
<*>
                        \item $D$, Deadline: How quickly must the task me completed after it
is started each time tick. $C < D < P$
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   return();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2.2 
\subsubsection{Intermittent Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in  all types of applications
        \item Examples: 1) Send an email every night at 4:00 AM\\
        2) Save all data when power is going down \\
        3) Send a message to plant operator when tank runs low.\\
        4) Calibrate a sensor on startup.

        \item Characterized by two attributes:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does it require each time.
                        \item $D$, Deadline: How quickly must the task be completed after it
is started. (whenever that happens to be).
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   if(! done) return();
   else halt_me();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 1.2.3 
\subsubsection{Background Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item A soft real time or non real time task.
        \item Lower Priority
        \item Will be accomplished only as CPU time is available when no
        hard real time tasks are ready.
        \item Characterized by:
                \begin{itemize}
                         \item $C$, Computing Resources: How much CPU time  does
                         it require each time between scheduler accesses.
                \end{itemize}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   compute for C seconds;
   if(! done) return();
   else halt_me();
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}



<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.2.4 
\subsubsection{Complex Tasks}
\begin{itemize}
        \item Found in all types of applications
        \item Examples: 1) Microsoft Word\\
        2) Apache Web Server.

        \item Characteristics:
                \begin{enumerate}
                        \item Continuous need for CPU time.
                        \item Frequent requests for I/O which free up the CPU.
                        \item Waits for user input which free up CPU.
                \end{enumerate}
         \item Typical Code Structure:
\begin{verbatim}
name() {
   while(1) {
      compute for C seconds;
      request_IO();  // also starts scheduler
      }
   }
\end{verbatim}
\end{itemize}
<*chn>
I/O activity frees up the CPU because the task must wait, usually a significant amount of
time, for the I/O activity on a physical device.  It cannot compute between the time it requests
the I/O and the time it gets the data or writes the data so now the CPU is free for other tasks.
<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 1.3
\subsection{Task States}\label{TaskStates}
Tasks are in one of four states:
\begin{enumerate}
        \item {\tt Running} 
        \item {\tt Ready} to Run (but not running)
        \item {\tt Waiting}    (for something {\it other} than the CPU.)
        \item {\tt Inactive}   
\end{enumerate}
<*chn>
Only one task can be {\bf Running} at a time (unless we are using a ``multicore" CPU).  A task which is waiting for the CPU 
is {\bf Ready}.  When a task has requested
I/O or put itself to sleep, it is {\tt Waiting}.
An {\tt Inactive} task is waiting to be allowed into the schedule.  It is like 
Microsoft Word when you are NOT running it. 

We'll see later how a task can get into or out of the {\tt Inactive} state.

<*>
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}




