%%%%** Section 3.5.2 
\subsubsection{Foreground/Background}
\begin{itemize}
        \item A mix of hard real-time periodic tasks with
        Soft real time or non real time intermittent and complex background tasks.
        \item ``Foreground" --- the hard real time tasks
        \item ``Background" --- the soft/non real time tasks
        \item Example: Factory Automation Controller:
        \begin{itemize}
                \item  Real time control system (periodic, hard
real-time, forground)
                \item  Hourly production report generator (intermittent, soft-real time, background)
                \item  Inventory Database Update (complex, non-realtime, background)
        \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
\begin{description}
\item[Exercise 7: Foreground/Background] Diagram the schedule for the
        following tasks:
        \begin{description}
                \item[A:] Periodic.  $P = 2$msec, $C = 233\mu$sec,
                           $D =500\mu$sec.
                \item[B:] Complex (background):
\begin{verbatim}
while(1){
  compute for 0.233ms;
  yield_to_scheduler();
  }
\end{verbatim}

\end{description}

({\tt yield\_to\_scheduler()} is similar to {\tt return()} in our earlier schedulers).
\end{description} 





<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.6
\subsection{Delay Functions and Scheduling}

Tasks frequently need to wait for a time interval.

Reasons:
\begin{itemize}
  \item To create time-based events (such as Flash an LED for 0.5 sec).
  \item To wait for something external to happen such as for a device to
           be ready for more data.
  \item To free up CPU time for other processes.
  \item To determine for themselves how often they run:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1)
  {
  compute;
  delay(20ms);  // Run every ~20ms.
  }
\end{verbatim}
This task sets its own period, $P$, to 20ms.
How accurate?

\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


The worst way to do this is a delay loop such as
\begin{verbatim}
#define DELAY    22000
for (i=0; i< DELAY ; i++) ;   // just loop to use up time
\end{verbatim}

<*chn>

Here we assume that we have figured out that 22000 is the right value to
use for the time delay we want by trial-and-error or calculation.   The
worst bad thing about this method is that it doesn't meet the third
reason, it uses all the CPU time itself.  (There are other problems as
well). We can define
the call {\tt OS\_TimeDelay(int delay)} which does the following:

A better method is to let the scheduler help with delays.

<*s>
The scheduler can help implement delays with a function:

{\tt OS\_TimeDelay(int delay)}:

<*>
\begin{itemize}
        \item returns to the scheduler
        \item sets a software counter to {\tt delay}.  
              (This counter will be decremented every so often by an ISR until {\tt counter == 0}.)        
        \item schedules the next task.
        \item the task which called {\tt OS\_TimeDelay()} will be set to 
         {\tt Waiting} (not started by the scheduler)
        \item when the {\tt delay } is over, set task to {\tt Ready}
\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 3.7
\subsection{Dynamic Scheduling}

<*chn>
\paragraph{Starting and Stopping}
Sometimes it is desireable to make a task stop and start. Recall the task states defined in 
Section \ref{TaskStates}.  We defined the
task state {\tt Inactive}, to describe a stopped task.  To implement
this, assume
that functions are available: {\tt halt\_me()} which moves a task from
{\tt Running} to
{\tt Inactive} states (takes a task out of the
scheduler's loop).  And {\tt run(task)} which moves a task from {\tt
Inactive} to {\tt Ready} (puts a task into the
scheduler loop).

<*s>
Why not allow tasks to start and stop?

Assume two functions:

 {\tt halt\_me().} Moves a task from {\tt Running}
to {\tt Inactive}

 {\tt start(task)}  Moves a task from {\tt Inactive}
 to {\tt Ready}.

<*>

\begin{description}
\item[Exercise 8: Dynamic Schedule] Diagram the following tasks:

\item [A] Intermittent:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1) {
  compute for 0.6566 ms;
  yield_to_scheduler();
  after 3 cycles of this halt_me();
  }
\end{verbatim}
    \item [B] Complex:  run for 1.0ms, then call the OS to do I/O which
      lasts for 2.0 ms.  Repeat.
    \item [C] Background:
\begin{verbatim}
while(1) {
  compute for 0.233 ms;
  yield_to_scheduler();
  }
\end{verbatim}

for
\begin{enumerate}
\item Non-synchronized, non-priority-based Round Robin
\end{enumerate}

\end{description}




<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}


%%%%** Section 3.8
\subsection{Pre-emptive Scheduling}

<*chn>
So far, we have required that tasks return to the scheduler frequently
so that other tasks get a chance to run in the CPU.  This has two
problems.  First, we have to code our tasks somewhat artificially.  They
have to break their job up into chunks and keep track.  Second, we are
vulnerable if some task doesn't ``play fair".

The solution to this is a scheduler that can break in to a running task
using a regular interrupt such as a clock interrupt.   There are many
complex issues in having this ability but the value is very great.
Preemptive schedulers solve this problem.  Whether the task is ready or
not, the pre-emptive scheduler can stop it and run something else.
Several types of pre-emptive scheduler are described below.

<*s>
\subsubsection*{Pre-emptive Scheduler can}
\begin{itemize}

   \item respond to a clock interrupt during a running task
   \item save context of the interrupted task
   \item look at the entire task list
   \item start another task (or possibly re-start the same task)
\end{itemize}

<*>

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.8.1 
\subsubsection{Types of pre-emptive schedulers:}

<chn>\begin{itemize}

<chn>        \item
Pre-emptive Round Robin
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Regular OS interrupts (``ticks'').
                \item Scheduler moves to next task each tick.
                \item \begin{verbatim}
Task()
{
while{1}
   { compute ; }
}
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>     \item
Priority Based
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Each task assigned priority by programmer
                \item Highest priority task always gets the CPU if
ready.                \item \begin{verbatim}
(same)
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item
 Rate Monotonic (RMS)
        \begin{itemize}
                \item As above but priorities are assigned based on
                period, $P$.
                \item Short period = high priority.
                \item Can prove optimality and schedulability (with
assumptions).      
                \item \begin{verbatim}
(same)
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

RMS Schedulability:

$C_i = $ execution time of task $i$.

$P_i = $ period of task $i$.

$n = $ number of tasks.

if 

\[
\sum^{n-1}_{i=0}\frac{C_i}{P_i} \leq n(2^{1/n}-1)
\]
the system {\bf is} schedulable using RMS.

Assumption:  All tasks pre-emptable 100\% of the time.
<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>        \item
$\mu$Cos-II style Priority Based
        \begin{itemize}
                \item Fixed unique priority and task-specific delay.
                \item Must tweak delay values, $M$,  to avoid
starvation.                \item \begin{verbatim}
Task()
  {
  compute_some();
  OSTimeDelay(M);
  }
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

<chn>       \item
 Priority Based Round Robin
        \begin{itemize}
                \item All tasks delay 1 tick
                \item All tasks execute $< \frac{T}{N}$ where $T$ is the
                OS tick period and $N$ is the number of tasks.
                \item  \begin{verbatim}
Task()
  {
  compute_a_little();
  OSTimeDelay(1);
  }
\end{verbatim}
        \end{itemize}

<chn>\end{itemize}

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\subsubsection*{Preemptive Scheduling Exercises}

\paragraph{Exercise 9:}
Diagram the following tasks:

\begin{description}
    \item [A] Intermittent: Run for 2ms and then {\tt halt\_me()}.
    \item [B] Complex:  run for 1.0ms, then call the OS to do I/O which
      lasts for 2.0 ms.  Repeat.
    \item [C] Background: Run forever.
\end{description}

for these policies:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Pre-emptive priority-based:  Priority order: 1) B, 2) A, 3) C. (1= highest)
\item Pre-emptive Round Robin (no priorities)
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Exercise 10:}
Assume 5 tasks and for each one P = 20ms, C=3ms, D=20ms. The OS
clock is 1ms. How could you set up delay values to implement
round-robin scheduling in $\mu$C/OS-II style Priority Based.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.9
\section{Scheduling Summary}


\begin{tabular}{|p{1.75in}|p{1.75in}|}
\hline
Issue                   & Scheduling Concepts    \\ \hline
How often will each task run? & Task types; Round Robin ; delay functions      \\ \hline
Which task runs next?         & Task State; Round Robin; Priority Schemes   \\\hline
How do tasks give up CPU?       & {\tt return()/yield()} ; Request I/O ;
                                       interrupts/preemption\\\hline
What if task doesn't need to run all the time?
                                & {\tt  dynamic scheduling ; halt\_me()/start(task)} \\\hline
How can we meet deadlines {\bf and} make full use of CPU?
                                & Background Tasks \\\hline
\end{tabular}


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

%%%%** Section 3.10
\section{RTOS Types}

{\tt
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|}
\hline
Type            & $C$               & $D$           & Scheduling        \\
\hline``Super HRT''   &  $\ll P$    & P             & Trivial           \\ \hline
``Hard RT''     &  $(1 \sim 5)T$        & $(10 \sim 20)T$        & Complex      \\ \hline
``Soft RT''     &  $(10 \sim 20)T$       & $\sim100T$    & Complex          \\ \hline
Non RT          &   X               & X             & Trivial           \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
}

<s> $P = $task period; $C =$ Compute Time; $D =$Deadline; $T =$ Time Quantum
<hn>Where $P,C,D,T$ are defined in sections \ref{Periodic} and \ref{Time}.

<s>\end{slide}

